                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Your house was designed &amp; constructed to be energy efficient and air tight. There was originally a Lifebreath Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed that provided air exchanges for the house, with exhaust from the kitchen/living room &amp; both bathrooms, together with fresh air supply throughout the house. At the time of my evaluation, the HRV had been decommissioned with a couple of changes:



1. The exhaust air from the kitchen/living area &amp; bathrooms is now interconnected into the return air plenum of the furnace.

2. The outside fresh air supply to the HRV has been moved &amp; connected to the return plenum of the furnace (right at the furnace location). I did see an adjustable damper on that fresh air supply pipe &amp; it was adjusted to the "fully closed" position.



During my evaluation, your daughter mentioned there seems to be excess moisture inside the house and it sometimes results in excess condensation buildup on the window ledges. I think this is a result of a few factors:

- The house is generally quite air tight

- The HRV has been disconnected

- The damper on the fresh air supply to the furnace is closed"

- Any humidity created in the bathrooms is just drawn back to the furnace &amp; recirculated inside the house

- The clothes dryer is venting inside the house (with some pantyhose to catch the lint)

- There is a humidifier in the master bedroom

- The kitchen range hood does vent to the outside but may not be used enough



As you can see from the list above, there are some operational changes that can be made to try &amp; reduce the excess humidity in the house.



For your higher efficiency performance house, it would ideally be good to have a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency (and ECM blower fan technology), designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, there is an estimated increase of $ 73 per year in energy costs - and likely worth it to ensure good indoor air quality. The operation of the HRV can be set up on a timer, rather than operating continuous - providing you with more control over the amount of ventilation the house &amp; occupants receives.



There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 189 litre electric hot water tank. Although hydro-electricity in Fort Smith is a GHG free fuel source, the cost of electricity has increased quite a bit in your area over the past 10 years. You are obviously already aware of "alternate fuel savings" for your hot water, as you were previously using an outdoor wood boiler to both heat your house (via a plenum coil) &amp; your domestic hot water (via a desuperheater loop). In the previous Heating System section of this report, I modeled a few options for your consideration.



Savings: The savings associated with this upgrade are captured in the previous Heating System upgrade section.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 86.6% efficient oil furnace that is drawing its' combustion air from inside the house and is vented up through a 5" chimney flue. There is also an older Wood Doctor wood boiler outside that you mentioned is not used now that your daughter lives in the house. You used to use the wood boiler to heat the house (via a hydronic coil in the supply plenum of the furnace) and also to heat your domestic hot water. It looks like there is also a loop off the wood boiler that goes through the basement wall and into the garage - likely heating the garage as well. The domestic hot water is now being provided with from a 13 year old, 189 litre electric hot water tank.



There are a few options that I wanted to model for you:



1. As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), I thought I would model the use of a 95% AFUE condensing propane combination boiler to both heat your house &amp; provide high efficiency on-demand hot water.



2. Staying with the current oil furnace, I modeled the savings if you were to replace the older Wood Doctor outdoor boiler with a newer biomass wood boiler that can achieve an 80% higher heating value efficiency for:

     - A) Heating the house using the boiler loop in the supply plenum

     - B) Also providing your domestic hot water via a desuperheater loop



For the above alternatives, I used our current Fuel Cost rates for Fort Smith, that are outlined in the Additional Comments section of this report. For the cost of wood, it looks like you will self-harvest, so I am using out discounted rate assumption of $100/cord (to cover your gas, chainsaw maintenance, use of a truck &amp; safety gear ...... &amp; assuming your labour cost is free).



Savings:



For #1 Above: You could potentially save $ 192 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



For #2A Above: You could potentially save $ 1,927 per year in heating energy costs and qualify for 50% of the cost of the new biomass boiler up to $ 20,000 (limited to a cost &amp; GHG savings calculation) from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



For 2B Above: You could potentially save $ 3,084 per year in combined heating &amp; hot water energy costs and qualify for 50% of the cost of the new biomass boiler up to $ 20,000 (limited to a cost &amp; GHG savings calculation) from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, there is a pre-approval process you will need to follow to determine how much funding your project may be eligible for. Program information, guidelines and applications are available on our website.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (only the kitchen range hood as the bathroom fans used to be part of the HRV exhaust (now disconnected) and the clothes dryer vents inside the house) indicated a pressure drop of 4.8 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is chimney vented and could be susceptible to back drafting if the negative pressure created in the house was greater than 5 Pa - which will easily happen once the clothes dryer is again vented outside.



Your current furnace draws its' combustion air from inside the house. The furnace upgrade option outlined previously would no longer require a chimney &amp; would draw its' combustion air from outside of the house.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors but did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $0.90 per litre 

Propane - $0.73

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.46 per bag, $299.62 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
